    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` following `spec.md`, keeping the given class name and method signatures. Each client keeps a time-ordered deque of `(time, cost)` entries, and entries older than `window_seconds` are dropped on each call. `allow` records only when `used + cost <= limit`, `remaining` is `limit - used`, and `retry_after` walks the live entries oldest-first to find when enough units expire. Argument validation rejects `bool` and non-integer `limit`/`cost`, and a global high-water mark enforces the no-backwards-time rule across all clients. I wrote 43 tests in `tests/test_solution.py` covering validation, the window boundary, weighted costs, rejected requests using no budget, client isolation, `retry_after` accuracy, and time monotonicity. Three of my initial test expectations were wrong, and I corrected those tests rather than the implementation. `python -m pytest -q tests` now passes with 43 tests. One ambiguity I resolved: a call that is rejected for budget reasons still advances the time high-water mark, since the spec says \"now seen so far\".",
